Problem: Sort a linked list of 0s, 1s and 2s (Dutch National Flag Problem)
Link: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/given-a-linked-list-of-0s-1s-and-2s-sort-it/1

Optimized Approach: single pass, O(1) space

Description:

Given the head of a linked list where nodes can contain values 0s, 1s, and 2s only. 
Your task is to rearrange the list so that all 0s appear at the beginning, followed by all 1s, and all 2s are placed at the end.

Examples:

Input: head = 1 → 2 → 2 → 1 → 2 → 0 → 2 → 2
   
Output: 0 → 1 → 1 → 2 → 2 → 2 → 2 → 2
Explanation: All the 0s are segregated to the left end of the linked list, 2s to the right end of the list, and 1s in between. The final list will be:
   
Input: head = 2 → 2 → 0 → 1
   
Output: 0 → 1 → 2 → 2
Explanation: After arranging all the 0s, 1s and 2s in the given format, the output will be:
   
Constraints:
1 ≤ no. of nodes ≤ 106
0 ≤ node->data ≤ 2

CODE:

/* Node is defined as
  class Node {
  public:
    int data;
    Node* next;

    Node(int x) {
        data = x;
        next = nullptr;
    }
};
*/
class Solution {
  public:
    Node* segregate(Node* head) {
        // code here
        //1st Approach:
        // Count the frequency of 0,1,2's while iterating the list
        // Perform second iteration to change the values of nodes
        
        // 2nd Approach: 
        // Other Approaches include, storing it in an array
        // i. Sort the Arr.
        // ii. Similarly count the frequency & accordingly address.
        
        // 3rd. In-Place: single pass, O(1) space
        if(!head) return NULL;

        Node* zeroHead = new Node(0); Node* zeroTail = zeroHead;
        Node* oneHead = new Node(0); Node* oneTail = oneHead;
        Node* twoHead = new Node(0); Node* twoTail = twoHead;
        
        Node* curr = head;
        while(curr) {
            Node* temp = curr;
            curr = curr->next;
            temp->next = NULL;  // Fix: Detach properly
            
            if(temp->data == 0) {
                zeroTail->next = temp;
                zeroTail = temp;
            } else if(temp->data == 1) {
                oneTail->next = temp;
                oneTail = temp;
            } else {
                twoTail->next = temp;
                twoTail = temp;
            }
        }
        
        // Chain: tails already at ends
        
        // if we have noOf1's > 0 then zeroTail->next = oneHead->next 
        // else it will connect to twoHead->next (skipping 0th dummy node of twoHead)
        zeroTail->next = (oneTail == oneHead ? twoHead->next : oneHead->next);
        
        //Similarly if noOf1's > 0 then one_end will store oneTail else zeroTail.
        Node* one_end = (oneTail == oneHead ? zeroTail : oneTail);  // If no 1s, end of 1s is end of 0s
        //Connecting zeroTail or oneTail to twoHead. 
        //Although the below step will always connect oneTail to twoHead because if noOf1's == 0
        //The connection would've already been established in the 1st step.
        one_end->next = twoHead->next;
        
        // Return real head, set final tail NULL
        
        //Iterate over the twoHead and set it's last node to point NULL;
        Node* twoTail_final = twoHead;
        while(twoTail_final->next) twoTail_final = twoTail_final->next;
        twoTail_final->next = NULL;
        
        //skipping zeroHead first dummy node.
        return zeroHead->next;

    }
};
